WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.280
[Narration over soft music] In 1932, the United&nbsp;
States Public Health Service began a study of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.920
the effects of untreated syphilis&nbsp;on&nbsp;
black men in Macon County, Alabama.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.600
Participants’ informed consent was not collected.&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:22.240
The study initially involved 600 black men – 399&nbsp;
with syphilis, 201 who did not have the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.240
Researchers told the men they were being treated&nbsp;
for “bad blood,” a local term used to describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:34.320
several ailments, including syphilis, anemia, and&nbsp;
fatigue. In exchange for taking part in the study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.800
the men received free medical exams,&nbsp;
free meals, and burial insurance.&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:46.000
Four decades later, on July 25, 1972,&nbsp;
Associated Press reporter Jean Heller broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.600
news about the study, prompting the U.S.&nbsp;
Department of Health, Education, and Welfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.720
to appoint an ad hoc panel to investigate.
In October 1972, the panel advised stopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:05.280
the study, and a month later the study was ended.
The panel concluded in their final report that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.098
the previous research on African Americans&nbsp;
with syphilis was “ethically unjustified.”&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.680
At the study's conclusion, only 74 of&nbsp;
the original 600 men were still alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.840
Forty of their wives had been infected and 19 of&nbsp;
their children were born with congenital syphilis.&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.840
The Advisory Panel’s investigation led to&nbsp;
compensation for the victims of the research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.240
and new protocols for the ethical&nbsp;
treatment of human subjects of research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.920
Voluntary informed consent for research subjects&nbsp;
became the new standard, and an explicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:46.080
requirement for government-funded research.
In 1997, President Clinton issued a formal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.200
Presidential apology for&nbsp;
the Tuskegee Syphilis Study.&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.440
During its investigation, the panel collected&nbsp;
a broad variety of historical documents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.862
other papers. In 1973, a copy of the panel’s&nbsp;
working papers was deposited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.862 --> 00:02:08.480
in the National&nbsp;Library of Medicine, NLM, located on the&nbsp;
campus of the National Institutes of Health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.240
in Bethesda, Maryland. Through this research&nbsp;
and related public education made possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.400
by the unique collection held by the NLM, as&nbsp;
well as the National Archives, we acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.000
the participants in the Tuskegee study and the&nbsp;
injustices they and their families suffered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.800
We also acknowledge the work of the Tuskegee&nbsp;
Syphilis Study Ad Hoc Advisory Panel to address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.720
the unethical actions of the study which ushered&nbsp;
in new protections for the rights of patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.040
and which continue to evolve with&nbsp;
new advances in science and medicine.&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:51.040
The NLM collects and preserves collections such as&nbsp;
that of the Tuskegee Study Ad Hoc Advisory Panel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:57.280
to improve transparency in research, and to&nbsp;
ensure that lessons of the past inform the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:03.840
present and future of biomedical research, health&nbsp;
care, public health, and healthy behavior.

00:03:04.275 --> 00:03:09.946
[Music fades to silence]

